@PP
What each solver does in detail depends on its own options; @F "rs"
only specifies when (if at all) it gets called, and how much time
it has to run, not what it does when it is called.  For example,
a solver may have an option that turns it off.  If that is set,
the solver does nothing.
@PP
The remarks about time limits in Section {@NumberOf general_solvers.yourself}
apply to resource solving.  There is an @F { rs_time_limit }
option for placing a time limit on resource assignment.  For example,
@ID @C {
rs_time_limit="10:0"
}
sets an overall time limit for resource assignment (including
repair) of 10 minutes.  Time weights may be used to apportion
the available time among the various solvers as usual.
@PP
The @F "rt <solver>" item calls @F "<solver>" once for each
resource type, raising the question of how the available time
is apportioned among the resource types.  It is apportioned
using the number of resources of each type as the time weight.
During setup, each @F "rt" item is converted into a sequence
of solver items, with one item for each resource type, whose
time weight is the number of resources in the resource type
and whose @F "<solver>" is @F { rt }'s @F "<solver>".
@PP
All of this is carried out by function
@ID @C {
bool KheCombinedResourceAssign(KHE_SOLN soln, KHE_OPTIONS options);
}
This sets the @C { rs_time_limit } time limit if that option is
present, then assigns resources using the value of option @C { rs }
as its guide, and finally deletes the time limit if it sets it.  The
easiest way to call it is to include @C { rs } in the @C { gs }
option (Section {@NumberOf general_solvers.general}), causing the
general solver to call it for you.
@PP
The @C { rs } option has default value
@ID @C {
gts!do rem rt(
  1: rin!do rrd rwp rcm rbw rah rgc rcx!rts,
  2: rin!do rgc (rfs, rrm, rec, rdv),
  1: (red, rrm, rec, rdv),
  *: re2
)
}
It is spread over several lines here for readability.  It
ends by tidying up any obvious deficiencies, using a call
to a shallow ejection chain solver (@C { re2 }).  This
is expected to take negligible time, so its time weight
has been given the special @C { * } value documented in
Section {@NumberOf general_solvers.yourself}.
@PP
The first line, enclosing everything, installs a separate global
tixel matching (high school timetabling only), creates an event
timetable monitor, then solves each resource type.
@PP
The second line is the construction phase.  It installs several
refinements:  the resource assignment invariant (high school
timetabling only), redundancy handling, workload packing, cluster
minimum limits, weekend balancing, assign by history, and grouping
by constraints.  It then does the actual construction, using
@C { rcx } (high school timetabling) or @C { rts } (nurse rostering).
@PP
The third line is the first repair phase.  After installing
fewer refinements, it proceeds to finding split assignments,
resource rematching, ejection chains, and VLSN search using
optimal reassignment by dynamic programming.
@PP
The third line is the second repair phase.  It repeats the
first repair phase, minus refinements and plus domain enlargement.
It is given half the running time of the first repair phase.
@PP
All this represents the author's current idea of how best to use
the various resource solvers.  It will change as his ideas change.
